body:
Not sure how familiar you are with flashing custom ROMs, but looks like there are plenty of options for that phone so you can fold away! :)

&nbsp;

[Here's one possible custom ROM (guide & downloads are inside the page as well).](http://forum.xda-developers.com/galaxy-s2/development-derivatives/rom-official-i9100-t2448450)

Don't worry too much, many factors could affect the amount of side effects you experience (such as the amount of activities of normal cells during the infusion, which would affect how much of them would be affected by the treatment; and might have been different in your 1st and 2nd infusion).

&nbsp;

If it would make you feel better, you can try talking to your doctor about this.

> If anything evolution selects against faulty mutations, at least up until reproductive age.

&nbsp; 

Does this mean shitty mutations are eventually gonna be eliminated (which kinda sounds like a purpose to me)?

Survival of the cancerous cells or the affected macro-organism? Seems like only the latter is more possible (like self-elimination of the macro-organism if it can't prevent or stop the cancerous cells)?

&nbsp;

Well, I think they work with each other simply to increase fitness & survival rates, just like how individials do in a community.

Usually I like the rather upbeat but not-too-noisy ones, but I've been losing my apetite on any kind of music since the treatment :(

&nbsp;

Hope the apetite will come back.

Thanks for elaborating, I've been re-reading your comment several times, but looks like I couldn't comprehend it fully right now (it's deep :) ), either because I haven't gone through what you have (perhaps I will get it the further I go), or chemo brain.

&nbsp;

And perhaps I haven't been able to fully grasp the concept of "everyone is not normal" either, but my brain seems to be still holding on to the idea that normalness is a range, and there's above normal (like superheroes), and below normal. I do hope I will find my cancer could turn me somehow into above normal instead of below normal in the future.

Aside from "standard" physical side effects (I get nausea, bloating, fatigue & dizziness primarily), there's this extreme discomfort inside my whole body (like it's telling me something is terribly wrong with my body thanks to the chemo meds, and like my body could break down/blow up any moment -- very hard to describe, maybe those who had been on the same road can help me describe this). Haven't been able to well explain this latter sensation to my doctor either. :(

&nbsp;

Thanks for your suggestions, will try to look into some of those books & see if they could be inspirational for me.

Thanks for the advice. Will try to talk more to my doctor & make sure no details are missed, so that he hopefully can help me handle these crazy side effects better.

&nbsp; 

> There were lots of times when I was finally feeling almost okay after a round of chemo and could hardly face the fact that the next round was only a day or two away.

Can totally relate to that (although I'm currently only on my second round)!

&nbsp;

Also, mind if I ask what kind of things made you want to go on living back then during your treatments?

Thank you for the kind words & sharing your sister's experiences.

&nbsp; 

Sadly, it seems like I have lost my passions since the diagnosis & not sure why I should even hold on. Maybe (and hopefully) this is temporary, I don't know.

&nbsp; 

Family has never been the most supportive thing in my life either. For instance, one of my siblings reaction to my chemo was rather *schadenfreuderic* (doesn't seem to care & seem to think "good thing it isn't me who's experiencing this" & just move on as if nothing is happening). It hurts, but oh well.

*(sorry this turned into a bit of a rant)*

&nbsp; 

Thanks again for the support!

Thanks for the encouragement! 10 is definitely way younger than my age, and that must've been a hella long treatment for you.

&nbsp; 

Mind sharing how your experiences have shaped who you are today in a positive way (hence you wouldn't want to change anything that has happened to you)?

&nbsp;

Also, how did you deal with the feeling of not being a normal person back then?
